        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>We could not do  a blower door test with the woodstove operating.  I suspect that most of the leakage that remains to be addressed is in the basement rim joists and basement pony wall

 </airSeal>
                <mainWalls>If you decide in the future to upgrade your siding then add insulation for sure.  The rebate for adding R20 to the walls is $2.50 per square foot. 

If you do upgrade the main walls then the chimneys should be boxed in to keep them warmer and allow for better draft.  Inspect the chimneys before closing them in in case they need replacement. </mainWalls>
                <foundation>Increase 90% ( just not around the wood stove and furnace) of the foundation concrete walls by R20 fo rincreased insulate 75% of the concrete fundation walls by R20 for increased comfort, Add R20 to the whole rim joist area. Upgrade that pony wall insulation in the basement to be the same thickness as the inreased insulation concrete wall.  The current rebate for R20 added is $2.50 per square foot.</foundation>
                <windows>Upgrade the windows to triple glazed plus 2 coats Low e and argon as you can afford them.  The biggest reason for this is comfort where windows are in good operating condition.  The current rebate for windows is $100 each </windows>
                <VentilationSystem>If you end up increasing your insulation everywhere and get the house air tight then it would be wise to add an HRV to maintain a healthy indoor environement</VentilationSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>The drainage away from the house is an important first step to take care of before attempting any basement renovations </AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
